# Analyses

Links above Total impact switch the results between analyses
(`useUI().analysis`, transient). Every analysis runs from the same plan; an
analysis only adds options to `calculate(household, refs, options)`. The
conversion schedule always comes from the plan's strategy as-is.

- **Retirement**: `calculate(household, refs)`, no options. Golden cases pin it.
- **Survivorship**: `options.death` = `survivorshipEvent(household)`: the saved
  `household.survivorship` (person + age, set in the tab) or the default (main
  person at 80). Needs two people.
- **Long-term care**: `options.longTermCare` = `longTermCareSettings(household)`
  (saved `household.longTermCare`, healed on read; default: main person, home
  care, 3%, starting at 80).
- **Disability**: `options.disability` = `disabilityEvent(household, start)`
  (saved `household.disability`, person + age; default: main person
  disabled this year). It can start before the plan start, like Survivorship. From January 1 of that year the
  person's salary and business income stop; their disability policies pay
  (see the insurance guide). Expenses don't change.

- **Mortgage analysis**: not a projection. `MortgageAnalysis.tsx` edits
  `household.mortgages` (statement fields) and shows `amortize()`
  (`lib/engine/amortization.ts`): monthly interest = balance x rate / 12 to
  the cent, payment left at 0 is worked out from the maturity date, a
  payment on the maturity date clears cent rounding, extra principal shows
  its savings. A lump sum (`lumpSum`, paid with the payment due in
  `lumpSumMonth` / `lumpSumYear`, missing or past: the next one, capped at
  what's owed) goes straight to principal once (row `lump`). With either
  prepayment, `amortize().choices` lists paying as scheduled, then extra
  monthly, lump sum and both (the ones that apply), and the summary's
  "Paying it down faster" table compares them. The lump sum is analysis
  only: Pay off or invest and Pay off from retirement strip it
  (`withoutLumpSum`), and `loanPaydown` never reads it. It never feeds `projectScenario`. Every mortgage entered on a
  property (`RealEstate.loans`, kind mortgage, balance > 0) with no statement
  linked by `loanId` (saved or deleted) shows up as one
  (`propertyMortgageStatements`); the first edit saves it, Remove saves then
  deletes it so it stays gone. ResultsView returns it before the tax tables load.

- **Car loan** (user's request): not a projection. `CarLoanAnalysis.tsx`
  edits `household.carLoan` (a `CarLoan`, the same `LoanStatement` fields as
  a mortgage, no escrow) with `setCarLoan`, and reuses Mortgage analysis's
  `PrepaymentFields`, `Summary` and `ScheduleTable` over `amortize()`.
  Missing, `carLoanFor` / `defaultCarLoan` (`lib/engine/carLoan.ts`) start
  from the Finance option in Lease vs finance: price less down payment, its rate,
  its term ending the last payment, payment worked out. Healed in
  `migrateHousehold` like a mortgage.

- **College** (user's request): not a projection. `CollegeAnalysis.tsx`
  edits `household.college` (a `CollegePlan`, one student) with
  `setCollege` and shows `compareCollegeOptions()` (`lib/engine/college.ts`,
  healed by `collegePlanFor`). Out of pocket saves in a brokerage account
  and a 529 saves the same: same deposits, same return. Each college year's
  bill (today's cost grown `costGrowth` a year from this year) is paid on
  January 1 from the account; what it can't cover is "from pocket". Deposits
  go in monthly, stop when college starts unless `contributeDuringCollege`.
  Brokerage growth pays `taxOnGrowth` each year end (so its leftover is tax
  free); the 529 saves `stateDeductionRate` on each deposit and its leftover
  earnings (pro-rata basis) owe `leftoverTaxRate` + 10%. Net cost = put in +
  from pocket - state tax saved - leftover after tax. Colors: out of pocket
  rust, 529 green.

- **Pay off or invest** (user's request): not a projection.
  `MortgagePayoffAnalysis.tsx` picks one Mortgage analysis entry (saved
  statements, then property mortgages via `propertyMortgageStatements`) and
  shows `compareMortgagePayoff()` (`lib/engine/mortgagePayoff.ts`) with
  `household.mortgagePayoff` (mortgage id, investment return, tax on growth;
  healed by `mortgagePayoffSettingsFor`, defaults 6% and 15%). Both choices
  spend the same each month: Pay it off invests each payment (plus extra
  principal) the loan would have taken; Keep it invests the principal balance
  and pays the loan. Monthly growth is `(1 + return x (1 - tax))^(1/12) - 1`.
  Compared at the loan's payoff month (from `amortize`). Breakeven return is
  the loan rate compounded monthly, grossed up for the tax. Escrow is left
  out, no interest deduction. Returns null for a loan that never pays off.

- **Pay off from retirement** (user's request): not a projection.
  `RetirementPayoffAnalysis.tsx` picks a mortgage (same list as Pay off or
  invest) and a tax-deferred account (`retirementPayoffAccounts`, largest
  first, starting at its entered balance) and shows `compareRetirementPayoff()`
  (`lib/engine/retirementPayoff.ts`) with `household.retirementPayoff`
  (account return, lump-sum and monthly tax rate, picked from
  `RETIREMENT_PAYOFF_TAX_RATES` 12/22/24/32/35%; defaults 6%, 32%, 22%).
  Pay it off takes balance / (1 - lump rate) today; Keep it takes each
  payment / (1 - monthly rate) that month. The account grows monthly at
  `(1 + return)^(1/12) - 1` either way; compared at the loan's payoff month.
  The bracket grid uses the closed form: payments carried to the end over
  (1 - monthly) minus the balance carried to the end over (1 - lump). Same
  rate both sides: payoff wins when the return is under the loan rate
  compounded monthly. Flags an account short of the lump sum or one that runs
  dry; RMDs, other draws, state tax and the early penalty are left out.
  Under the Summary and Year by year tables, a "What each column means"
  list (user's request, `ColumnDefinitions` with `SUMMARY_COLUMN_DEFINITIONS`
  and `YEAR_COLUMN_DEFINITIONS`) says in plain words what every column is
  and how it's figured. Pay off or invest has the same two lists. Keep them
  in step when a column changes.

- **Social Security analysis**: not a projection. `SocialSecurityAnalysis.tsx`
  edits `household.socialSecurityClaims` (person, claiming age, monthly
  benefit at that age; at most `MAX_SOCIAL_SECURITY_CLAIMS`, 2, per person,
  the first two entered) and shows `socialSecurityBreakeven()`
  (`lib/engine/socialSecurity.ts`). The first option is the plan's own Social
  Security income (its start year and amount); every option grows by that
  income's COLA (`growthRate`) from its own claim year, full years, before
  tax, not discounted, through the person's final age. The crossover year is
  the first year an option's running total reaches the plan's (from either
  side). It never feeds `projectScenario`; ResultsView returns it before the
  tax tables load.

- **Medicare analysis**: reads the Retirement run (`calculate(household,
  refs)`, no options) and skips the results tabs, returned after the tax
  tables load. `MedicareAnalysis.tsx` shows `medicareYears()`
  (`lib/engine/medicare.ts`) for both scenarios: a Lifetime Medicare table
  (Part B premiums, the IRMAA part, years with IRMAA, and the conversion's
  impact, each as a lifetime total and an average a month over the months
  anyone is on Medicare) and a Medicare by year table for one scenario (With
  conversion by default). Every premium column there is monthly (user's
  request), and every table, the IRMAA tables included, has a "What each
  column means" guide under it (`ColumnGuide`). Each year's premium uses the MAGI from two years earlier (the
  first two years use the first year's); MAGI is `grossTaxableIncome +
  capitalGainsIncome`. Room to next tier is how much more of this year's
  MAGI fits before the premium two years later moves up (under the filing
  status of that year), and Next tier costs is that step for everyone
  enrolled then. `medicareTotal` in `runScenario.ts` is `medicareYears(...)
  .totalPremiums`, so the comparison's Medicare metric and this tab always
  agree. No saved fields. A "Show the full <year> IRMAA tables" link under
  the intro (user's request) opens every tier for single / head of household
  and married filing jointly from `refs.medicare`: MAGI range, monthly
  premium per person, and the IRMAA part monthly and yearly.

- **Spending capacity** (user's request): its own projections, skips the
  results tabs, returned after the tax tables load. `calculateSpendingCapacity`
  (`lib/calculate.ts`) builds the plan's schedule once, then
  `spendingCapacity()` (`lib/engine/spendingCapacity.ts`) searches, for each
  scenario, the largest `ProjectionOptions.spendingScale` (multiplies only
  `household.expenses`, not care or property loans) for which every row's
  surplus is at least -$1 a month with `coverShortfalls` on (the same
  after-tax, tax-deferred, Roth top-up as an event year, `topup:` income) and
  the last row's accounts (retirement + Roth + after-tax, before tax) are at
  least `household.spendingCapacity.endReserve`. Doubling then halving to
  within $1 a month of first-year spending. A surplus year isn't saved (the
  engine never saves surplus), so the answer leans safe. Retirement runs
  never set either option, so golden cases don't move.

- **Monte Carlo** (user's request): its own projections, skips the results
  tabs, returned after the tax tables load. `MonteCarloAnalysis.tsx` builds
  the schedule and the 1,000 market paths once per plan, runs them in
  slices (`monteCarloRun`, both scenarios on the same path), then
  `summarizeMonteCarlo`: success rate (every year paid through the final
  age, short years covered from the accounts), how often converting leaves
  the bigger inheritance, 10th / median / 90th percentile inheritance and
  after-tax assets at the RMD age, and accounts left by year for the range
  chart. The plan on its own returns (`monteCarloPlan`) is shown alongside.
  Engine rules are in AGENTS.md, "Monte Carlo". No saved fields.

- **Lease vs finance**: not a projection. `CarAnalysis.tsx` edits
  `household.carDecision` and shows `compareCarOptions()`
  (`lib/engine/carDecision.ts`): pay cash, finance (down payment, APR, term)
  or lease (monthly, term, due at signing, turn-in fee) over `years`. Bought
  cars depreciate `depreciationRate` a year and count their value less any
  loan owed at the end; a lease shorter than the comparison is renewed on the
  same terms, and one still running at the end counts only its months
  inside. Lease mileage: miles over `leaseMilesPerYear` (from
  `milesDrivenPerYear`) cost `excessMileRate` each, paid at each turn-in
  (prorated for a lease still running). The tables color each option (cash
  blue, finance rust, lease green). "Net cost with lost growth" carries every payment forward at
  `investmentReturn`. Defaults (`defaultCarDecision`) take the price from a
  one-time depreciation entry named like a car. Before income tax.

## Survivorship rules (`domain/survivorship.ts` + `projectScenario`)

A death (and, in Disability, a disability) can be any year from today on;
the lists read "2028 · age 55". When the event comes before the plan start
(first retirement year), `analysisStartYear` starts the projection in that
year (never before `asOfDate`'s year) and runs to the same final
age. Start-year amounts (incomes, expenses, rent, care) stay anchored to the
plan start: before it they hold at the entered amount. The conversion
schedule is per plan year, so earlier years convert nothing. Accounts,
property and business values compound from today to the earlier start;
mortgages amortize from it. ResultsView keys the snapshot by the first row's
year so Jump to resets.

The person passes at the end of `birthYear + deathAge`; effects start the
next year:
- filing status mfj becomes single (row `filingStatus`), senior deduction and
  Medicare count only `livingIds`,
- life-only pensions of the deceased stop, survivorship pensions keep paying
  the same amount (`pensionPayout`),
- Social Security: the survivor keeps the larger of the two benefits; the
  tab notes a step-up (`socialSecurityAfterDeath`) or that none happens,
- the deceased's salary, business and disability insurance income stop;
  withdrawals and other income keep running,
- accounts pass to the survivor (`effectiveOwner`): RMDs and conversion
  eligibility follow the survivor's age.
- expenses drop to `SURVIVOR_EXPENSE_SHARE` (70%) of the plan's amounts.
Inheritance uses the final row's filing status.

## Lifetime income from the accounts once an event happens

Survivorship (from the year after the death) and Long-term care (from the
first care year) turn every investment and retirement account (after-tax,
tax-deferred, Roth; never business or real estate) into a fixed lifetime
income, the same way life insurance proceeds work: the balance that
January times `lifeInsurancePayoutRate` (the Transamerica "Immediate
Payout %" column; under 59 is 5%, past 86 holds) for the age of the person
it pays (the owner, the survivor after a death, the younger spouse on a
joint account). From then the account grows 8%
(`LIFE_INSURANCE_PROCEEDS_GROWTH`) instead of its own rate and pays the
income first (step 2b). Guaranteed: past an empty balance it keeps paying,
taxed like the account's money (tax-deferred ordinary, after-tax as gains
vs basis, Roth free). The income counts toward the RMD: only an RMD above it
is added. Income line `draw:<accountId>` (`eventWithdrawalKey`), labelled
"Supplemental LTC income · <account>" in Long-term care and "Additional
survivorship income · <account>" in Survivorship (`incomeLabel` with
`longTermCare`, from `hasCareCosts` on the run's rows), charted as
Withdrawals from assets. The Articles line's "Generated income definitions"
pop-up (`components/plan/IncomeDefinitions.tsx`) explains every income the
plan adds on its own, reading the engine's constants; keep it in step when
one of these rules changes.

A year still short after that income (step 5c) takes more from the same
accounts until the surplus is 0 or they're empty: after-tax first, then
tax-deferred, then Roth, grossed up for the tax it adds (fixed point on
`priceYear`; the rental-loss allowance keeps its pre-draw MAGI). Line
`topup:<accountId>` (`eventTopUpKey`), "Extra withdrawal · <account>". A
year with more income than spending keeps the surplus. Disability doesn't
do any of this (yet).

Survivorship, Disability and Long-term care show only the no-conversion
scenario (`single` in ResultsView): one snapshot table, no No/With toggles,
no conversion tax switch, and Summary shows `ScenarioMetrics` (the six totals)
instead of Total impact. Retirement still compares the two.

## Long-term care rules (`domain/longTermCare.ts` + `projectScenario`)

- One spouse or both. Each person in care has a start age (default 80) and a
  length in years (default 3 for men, 5 for women, 3 when `Person.sex` isn't
  set), both editable in the tab.
- Cost per person, chosen per person (`CarePeriod.careType`, falling back to
  the settings' `careType` for older plans): home care $6,000/mo or nursing home $10,000/mo in
  first-projection-year dollars, grown by `inflation` (default 3%) each year.
  It lands in `expenseMonthly` under `ltc:<personId>` (labelled by
  `expenseLabel`) and in `monthlyExpenses`.
- Regular expenses while someone is in care, as a share of the pre-care
  amounts (`CARE_EXPENSE_SHARES`, user's table; order of spouses doesn't
  matter): one spouse in home care 75%, one in a nursing home 60%, both in
  home care 55%, one home and one nursing 50%, both in a nursing home 0%.
  That table is for couples. A one-person household has its own
  (`INDIVIDUAL_CARE_EXPENSE_SHARES`, user's numbers): home care 70%, nursing
  home 0%, since nobody is left at home. Back to 100% after care ends. Survivorship's 70% multiplies in
  if both analyses' options are ever combined.
- The tab opens with a note saying so (the user asked for it on top).
- Main opens with two cards above the Assets, Income & Taxes table (user's
  request, `LongTermCareImpact.tsx`, numbers from `longTermCareAssetImpact`
  in `lib/engine/longTermCareImpact.ts`): how much total assets went down
  from the year-end before care to the end of the last care year (first stay
  to last stay), and how much less that is than the same plan without care
  (the Retirement run's no-conversion scenario) at that year-end. Either can
  flip to "went up" / "More than" when growth outpaces the care costs.
  A third card, Cash flow during care (user's request), says Sufficient
  as long as the care gets paid, from income or from savings: years where
  income alone fell short and the top-ups (`topup:*` income) covered it are
  listed with what they took. It's Not sufficient only once the accounts run
  dry and spending is left unpaid (a negative surplus), per the user.
  It also says the year savings run out (`savingsRunOutYear`: the first
  unpaid year from the start of care to the plan's end, since top-ups keep
  running after care), or "Savings last through" the last plan year.
- Care costs aren't deducted as medical expenses (no itemizing in the model).
- LTC rider (user's request): a switch on the tab (`rider`) plus a checkbox
  per account (`riderAccountIds`, missing means every account), healed in
  `longTermCareSettings`, resolved to `ResolvedCare.riderAccountIds` via
  `hasCareRider`. In the years its owner is in care (either
  spouse for a joint account), an account with the rider pays
  `LTC_RIDER_INCOME_MULTIPLIER` (2x) the lifetime income and what's left
  grows `LTC_RIDER_GROWTH` (4%) instead of 8%. After care it's back to the
  normal income and 8% (user's call). The doubled amount counts toward the
  RMD those years. Off by default, so older plans don't change.

## Results layout (what clients see first)

Approved by the user: keep it (see AGENTS.md, "Results layout").

Under the analysis links, four tabs (`ResultsView` state, Main on load);
each tab shows only its own sections:
- **Main**: the Assets, Income & Taxes tables.
- **Summary**: Total impact, the metric cards, and After-tax assets. Taxes
  show as lifetime only; the before and after RMD age split opens from a
  "Show taxes before and after" link under the cards.
- **Graphic view**: `PresentationCharts` plus the Assets / Cash flow charts.
- **Main in detail analysis**: the Year-by-year table, alone in the scroller
  for pin-to-top.
The No conversion / With conversion toggle sits on Year-by-year and on the
Assets and cash flow charts (shared state).

## Jump to from today

ResultsView runs the plan twice: the normal run (Summary totals) and a
display run with `options.fromYear` = this year for Main, Graphic view and
Year-by-year, so Jump to reaches back to today. Amounts, property and
business values stay anchored to the plan start (before it they hold at
today's values), conversions only run in plan years, so from the plan start
on the display run matches the normal one (engine test pins it; mortgages
amortize from the earlier year). The tables still open on the plan start.
